  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp decline followed by a spike (e.g., Example 1 and 2), the model systematically underestimates the immediate post-spike recovery, failing to capture volatility expansion.",
    "The model exhibits a strong mean-reversion bias in the first 5-7 days of the forecast horizon, predicting a slow, linear drift toward historical averages instead of the observed V-shaped or U-shaped rebounds seen in true outcomes.",
    "Sentiment history is largely ignored by the model; even when sentiment is neutral (0.00) during volatile periods, the model fails to adjust its trajectory based on market stress signals present in the price history.",
    "In low-volatility regimes (Example 5), the model correctly predicts a slow upward drift but still underestimates the magnitude of sudden mean-reversion events that occur after an initial rally.",
    "The forecast horizon is too long for short-term volatility; corrections should be applied by reducing the predicted growth rate in the first week and increasing variance assumptions when recent 18-day history shows high dispersion."
  ]
}
